======
GENCAN
======

Gencan solves

    Minimize f(x)   subject to   l <= x <= u

(or unconstrained problems, through the entry point genunc) by an
active-set method whose inner step is a truncated Newton direction
computed with the conjugate gradient method. The user provides f, its
gradient and, optionally, its sparse Hessian.

Files
-----

    sources/gencan.f90     the solver (genunc and gencan)
    sources/cgm.f90        the conjugate gradient method
    sources/lssit.f90      Hessian-vector products and preconditioner
    sources/toyprob.f90    example: generalized Rosenbrock, n variables
    sources/cutestma.f90   driver for CUTEst problems
    compile.sh             builds the example
    run-cutest.sh          runs CUTEst problems
    problems.txt           475 unconstrained / box-constrained CUTEst problems


===========
THE EXAMPLE
===========

    ./compile.sh
    ./toyprob

toyprob.f90 solves the generalized Rosenbrock function twice: without
bounds (genunc) and with the box -2 <= x <= 0.5 (gencan). It is the
place to start: it shows the three routines you have to write --
evalf, evalg and evalh -- and the two calls to the solver. The
Hessian is given in sparse coordinate format, lower triangle only.
Change the parameter n at the top of the file to run larger instances.


============================
SOLVING PROBLEMS FROM CUTEST
============================

If the variables CUTEST, SIFDECODE, MASTSIF and MYARCH are already set
in your environment, nothing needs to be edited. Otherwise, uncomment
the four export lines at the top of run-cutest.sh and write your paths
there. Then:

    ./run-cutest.sh                 all the problems in problems.txt
    ./run-cutest.sh ROSENBR         one problem
    ./run-cutest.sh -f mylist.txt   the problems in mylist.txt, one name per line

The output of the solver goes to the screen. Setting table=1 in
run-cutest.sh also appends one line per problem to table-gencan.txt:

    Problem  SC  IERR  CPU(s)  n  f  gpsupn  iter  fcnt  gcnt  hcnt

    SC      stopping code: 0 = converged (||projected gradient||_inf <= 1e-8),
            99 = CPU-time limit reached (tlim in run-cutest.sh, default 600 s)
    gpsupn  sup-norm of the projected gradient at the final point
    fcnt, gcnt, hcnt   number of calls to evalf, evalg, evalh

To build a list of problems, copy the names you want from problems.txt
into a file, one per line; lines starting with # are ignored.


====================================
TRUE HESSIAN OR SECANT APPROXIMATION
====================================

The solver has two modes, chosen by the logical truehp (set in
toyprob.f90 and in cutestma.f90, default .true.):

    truehp = .true.    Newton-CG with the true Hessian: evalh is called
                       and the CG solves a genuine sparse linear system.
    truehp = .false.   the Hessian-vector product is replaced by a
                       secant approximation built from function and
                       gradient values; evalh is never called.

For work on the linear solver itself (e.g. a CG on GPU) the first mode
is the relevant one.


===============
FLOW OF CONTROL
===============

    genunc / gencan  (gencan.f90)
        |
        |  at each iteration: chooses the free variables, then asks
        |  for a Newton direction on them
        v
    cgm  (cgm.f90)
        |
        |  solves  H d = -g  on the free variables by conjugate
        |  gradients; at each CG iteration it needs two operations
        v
    computehv, applyhpre  (lssit.f90)
        |
        |  computehv:  the product H v.  With truehp = .true. it uses
        |              the sparse Hessian stored in lssit (arrays hrow,
        |              hcol, hval, filled by evalh); with .false. it
        |              uses the secant approximation.
        |  applyhpre:  application of the preconditioner.
        v
    evalf, evalg, evalh  (your routines)

gencan.f90 calls the user routines for f and its gradient, and hands
evalh to lssit, which stores the Hessian and provides the products.
cgm.f90 never sees the user routines nor the matrix: it only calls
computehv and applyhpre.


==================================
WHERE THE CONJUGATE GRADIENT LIVES
==================================

The whole CG is the subroutine cgm in sources/cgm.f90, about 300
lines. To replace it by another linear solver, keep its interface and
rewrite its body; gencan.f90 does not need to change. In the
truehp = .true. mode the Hessian is available inside lssit as a sparse
matrix in coordinate format (hrow, hcol, hval, lower triangle), which
is what a GPU kernel would consume.


=============
SCREEN OUTPUT
=============

The amount of output is set by the constant iprint at the top of
gencan.f90 (edit and recompile):

    iprint =  0   nothing
    iprint =  2   one line every iprintstep iterations plus the stopping
                  reason (the default)
    iprint =  3   one line per iteration
    iprint = 10   everything: the line search, the face decisions, the
                  tolerance and iteration limit of each CG call, and one
                  line per CG iteration

The line per CG iteration at level 10 is the place to look when working
inside cgm.f90.
